WEBVTT

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:04.608
The company's HR bot started with ten lines.

00:00:04.908 --> 00:00:08.724
A friendly tone, a couple of rules, and that was it.

00:00:09.374 --> 00:00:12.160
Today, its system prompt looks like this.

00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:15.727
Forty paragraphs, about four thousand tokens.

00:00:16.027 --> 00:00:18.704
And there are three things worth noticing.

00:00:19.354 --> 00:00:22.151
First, the remote work rule appears twice.

00:00:22.451 --> 00:00:24.499
Once it says three days a week.

00:00:25.149 --> 00:00:27.334
And further down, it says two.

00:00:27.634 --> 00:00:31.087
With different values, and nobody deleted the old one.

00:00:31.737 --> 00:00:37.238
Second, the prompt asks for brevity, at the very end of a four thousand token text.

00:00:37.888 --> 00:00:41.339
And third, half the content isn't instruction at all.

00:00:41.639 --> 00:00:42.617
It's knowledge.

00:00:42.917 --> 00:00:46.360
The vacation policy, the reimbursement policy.

00:00:46.660 --> 00:00:51.798
In this lesson, you'll learn to recognize this smell, why it grows, what it costs,

00:00:51.798 --> 00:00:54.743
and how to shrink it without breaking anything.

00:00:55.943 --> 00:00:59.180
That last point is the key to the diagnosis.

00:00:59.830 --> 00:01:04.572
An instruction tells the model how to act, in any conversation.

00:01:04.872 --> 00:01:05.630
Be brief.

00:01:05.930 --> 00:01:07.634
Never discuss salaries.

00:01:08.284 --> 00:01:11.084
Knowledge is the subject of some conversations.

00:01:11.384 --> 00:01:15.983
The vacation policy only matters when someone asks about vacation.

00:01:16.283 --> 00:01:22.451
But when knowledge lives in the system prompt, it rides along on every single call.

00:01:22.751 --> 00:01:24.966
Including the ones that don't need it.

00:01:25.616 --> 00:01:27.454
So how do you recognize it?

00:01:27.754 --> 00:01:32.323
The system prompt is past a few thousand tokens, and it only grows.

00:01:32.973 --> 00:01:35.950
Every fixed bug became a new paragraph.

00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:38.532
Never the removal of an old one.

00:01:39.182 --> 00:01:43.524
Some rules contradict each other, and nobody knows which one wins.

00:01:44.174 --> 00:01:46.296
Whole blocks of knowledge are pasted in.

00:01:46.596 --> 00:01:50.295
Policies, catalogs, frequently asked questions.

00:01:50.945 --> 00:01:55.259
And nobody on the team can explain what each paragraph does.

00:01:55.559 --> 00:02:00.160
Changing one line changes answers about topics that have nothing to do with it.

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:04.126
Nobody writes a bloated prompt on purpose.

00:02:04.426 --> 00:02:08.209
It grows out of reasonable decisions, one at a time.

00:02:08.509 --> 00:02:10.278
And it grows in a cycle.

00:02:10.928 --> 00:02:13.808
A case shows up that the bot handles badly.

00:02:14.458 --> 00:02:18.877
The quickest fix, at six in the evening, is adding one more instruction.

00:02:19.177 --> 00:02:20.451
Do not do this.

00:02:21.101 --> 00:02:25.542
The prompt grows, and every new instruction competes with the old ones.

00:02:26.192 --> 00:02:30.094
The model follows the existing rules a little less reliably.

00:02:30.394 --> 00:02:31.545
New bugs appear.

00:02:31.845 --> 00:02:33.814
And the cycle starts over.

00:02:34.464 --> 00:02:36.817
Adding is faster than understanding.

00:02:37.467 --> 00:02:38.828
There are no evals.

00:02:39.128 --> 00:02:44.418
Without a test set, removing a paragraph is scary, because nobody knows what will

00:02:44.418 --> 00:02:44.810
break.

00:02:45.460 --> 00:02:47.556
The prompt is the only place.

00:02:47.856 --> 00:02:52.906
With no search index and no tools, every piece of information ends up there.

00:02:53.556 --> 00:02:55.217
And several people edit it.

00:02:55.517 --> 00:02:58.865
Each one adds their rule, without reading everyone else's.

00:03:00.065 --> 00:03:02.296
Now the cost of ignoring it.

00:03:02.596 --> 00:03:05.894
Let's start with money, using some illustrative numbers.

00:03:06.194 --> 00:03:11.131
A four thousand token prompt, times twenty thousand messages a day, is eighty

00:03:11.131 --> 00:03:12.863
million input tokens a day.

00:03:13.513 --> 00:03:18.231
At a hypothetical one dollar per million, that's about two thousand four hundred

00:03:18.231 --> 00:03:20.413
dollars a month, just for the prompt.

00:03:20.713 --> 00:03:23.933
Before the user has typed a single word.

00:03:24.583 --> 00:03:25.520
Then time.

00:03:25.820 --> 00:03:31.517
Bigger input means slower answers, especially before the first word shows up.

00:03:32.167 --> 00:03:33.050
Then quality.

00:03:33.350 --> 00:03:36.178
Instructions compete for the model's attention.

00:03:36.478 --> 00:03:41.416
Research has shown that models make worse use of what sits in the middle of a long

00:03:41.416 --> 00:03:41.898
context.

00:03:42.198 --> 00:03:48.002
An important rule in paragraph twenty-three of forty tends to be followed less

00:03:48.002 --> 00:03:48.970
consistently.

00:03:49.270 --> 00:03:54.858
And rules that contradict each other produce answers nobody can predict.

00:03:55.508 --> 00:03:56.643
And team speed.

00:03:56.943 --> 00:03:58.506
Nobody wants to touch it.

00:03:58.806 --> 00:04:03.447
Every change becomes a session of manual testing, and hoping.

00:04:04.647 --> 00:04:06.111
Here's one for you.

00:04:06.411 --> 00:04:10.413
With that prompt, an employee asks how many remote days they get.

00:04:10.713 --> 00:04:12.873
Sometimes the bot says three.

00:04:13.173 --> 00:04:15.186
Sometimes it says two.

00:04:15.486 --> 00:04:17.452
What's the most likely cause?

00:04:20.502 --> 00:04:25.358
The prompt has two contradicting rules, and the model picks one each time.

00:04:25.658 --> 00:04:26.854
It's not temperature.

00:04:27.154 --> 00:04:30.494
Even at zero, the contradiction would still be there.

00:04:30.794 --> 00:04:33.424
And it's not that the model misunderstood.

00:04:33.724 --> 00:04:35.208
The question is simple.

00:04:35.508 --> 00:04:38.283
The problem is in what the model receives.

00:04:38.933 --> 00:04:41.913
The fix is keeping each rule in exactly one place.

00:04:42.213 --> 00:04:44.966
Not asking the model to pay more attention.

00:04:46.166 --> 00:04:50.077
There are three refactorings, depending on what the bloat is made of.

00:04:50.727 --> 00:04:55.272
If the prompt handles several topics or tasks, extract subprompt.

00:04:55.572 --> 00:04:59.039
Each one gets its own call, often behind a router.

00:04:59.689 --> 00:05:03.966
If the bloat is style and format rules, replace rules with examples.

00:05:04.266 --> 00:05:08.627
A few good examples teach more than twenty rules in capital letters.

00:05:09.277 --> 00:05:14.713
And if the bloat is content, like policies, catalogs and FAQs, move it out of the

00:05:14.713 --> 00:05:15.182
prompt.

00:05:15.482 --> 00:05:20.247
That content goes to an index, and comes in through retrieval only when it's

00:05:20.247 --> 00:05:20.811
relevant.

00:05:21.461 --> 00:05:26.190
Apply all three to the HR bot, and the system prompt shrinks to four lines.

00:05:26.490 --> 00:05:28.949
Answer from the policy excerpts.

00:05:29.249 --> 00:05:33.546
If it's not there, say you don't know and point to the HR channel.

00:05:33.846 --> 00:05:35.550
Never discuss salaries.

00:05:35.850 --> 00:05:38.915
And answer in at most three sentences.

00:05:39.565 --> 00:05:42.971
Notice that be brief became a limit you can check.

00:05:43.271 --> 00:05:45.086
At most three sentences.

00:05:45.736 --> 00:05:50.479
The policies become documents in an index, each with an effective date.

00:05:50.779 --> 00:05:55.835
The three day rule simply disappears, because only the current version is indexed.

00:05:56.135 --> 00:06:01.396
And a reimbursement question brings in only the reimbursement excerpt.

00:06:02.046 --> 00:06:07.621
But before any of it, build an eval with real questions, and measure the current

00:06:07.621 --> 00:06:08.109
prompt.

00:06:08.409 --> 00:06:11.620
That's what gives you the courage to delete paragraphs.

00:06:12.270 --> 00:06:17.551
The common mistake here is cutting by gut feeling, on a Saturday, with no eval.

00:06:17.851 --> 00:06:23.020
A paragraph that looked useless might be the one holding a rare case, like the rule

00:06:23.020 --> 00:06:23.767
for interns.

00:06:24.067 --> 00:06:29.806
Cut one block at a time, run the eval, and keep going only if the numbers hold.

00:06:31.006 --> 00:06:32.604
And when can you live with it?

00:06:32.904 --> 00:06:34.384
A prototype.

00:06:34.684 --> 00:06:39.509
To validate an idea in a week, a big messy prompt can be the fastest path.

00:06:39.809 --> 00:06:42.099
Just don't take it to production that way.

00:06:42.749 --> 00:06:44.748
Low volume, stable content.

00:06:45.048 --> 00:06:50.520
An internal tool used ten times a day, with a base that barely changes, can live

00:06:50.520 --> 00:06:51.751
with a big prompt.

00:06:52.051 --> 00:06:54.197
Turn on caching, and move on.

00:06:54.847 --> 00:07:00.222
And a prompt that's long because it holds eight well chosen examples isn't bloated.

00:07:00.522 --> 00:07:05.215
If accuracy is high and nobody's touched it in months, it's healthy.

00:07:05.515 --> 00:07:09.002
The smell is growth without criteria, not size.

00:07:09.652 --> 00:07:11.042
One last trap.

00:07:11.342 --> 00:07:16.106
Someone turns on prompt caching, the cost of the eight thousand token prompt drops a

00:07:16.106 --> 00:07:18.375
lot, and they say the problem is solved.

00:07:18.675 --> 00:07:21.434
Caching lowers the price of repeated tokens.

00:07:21.734 --> 00:07:25.908
But the model still reads the whole prompt, on every call, with the same

00:07:25.908 --> 00:07:29.097
contradictions, and the same competition for attention.

00:07:30.297 --> 00:07:32.283
So here's the idea to take with you.

00:07:32.583 --> 00:07:37.681
A system prompt should hold how to behave, not everything the bot might ever need to

00:07:37.681 --> 00:07:37.984
know.

00:07:38.284 --> 00:07:44.485
Each rule in one place, knowledge in an index, and an eval so you can delete with

00:07:44.485 --> 00:07:45.327
confidence.

00:07:45.627 --> 00:07:50.797
A prompt someone can read in two minutes is a prompt your team can change.

00:07:51.447 --> 00:07:56.741
In the next lesson, we'll move part of what the prompt asks for out of the text, and

00:07:56.741 --> 00:08:00.207
into a contract your code can check: structured output.

00:08:00.507 --> 00:08:01.337
See you there.
